  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run or build it here, so none of it has been checked in a browser or against the 20-second cycle.\n\n- **Island:** one procedural mesh with flat-shaded faceted terrain and per-face colours (sand, grass, rock, snow). Low-poly pine trees (a trunk and two cones each) and rocks are merged into the same mesh. It is about 56 m across and framed by the camera.\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL surface with vertex waves, faceted shading, a lighter shallow tint and animated foam at the shoreline. It also has a sun or moon glint and a sky-coloured horizon fade. Its uniforms update every frame.\n- **Day/night:** the cycle is 20 s. The Preetham sky (with stars) and a shadow-casting sun both follow the sun's path, and a moon light and hemisphere fill are driven from the same state. The hemisphere fill never drops below 0.6 intensity, so the scene stays lit at night.\n- **Camera:** it sits 15 m above the water at a 46 m radius and orbits the island once per minute, with the horizon in view.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one animation frame.\n\nThe camera orbit relies on `app.camera?.setPose` and the light changes rely on `setLight`. Both are called with optional chaining. If either isn't available at runtime, the camera stays still or light intensity and colour stop changing, but the scene should still render.\n\nThe water's horizon colour is an approximation of the Preetham sky's horizon, so a slight colour band at the horizon is possible.",
